package settings

import (
	"net/url"
	"time"
)

// LegacySettings configures the legacy Bitcoin wire protocol service.
type LegacySettings struct {
	WorkingDir                            string        `key:"legacy_workingDir" desc:"Working directory for legacy operations" default:"../../data" category:"Legacy" usage:"Path for legacy data storage" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the directory where the legacy Bitcoin protocol service stores its operational data.\n\n### How It Works\nThis directory contains:\n- **Peer database** - Known legacy node addresses\n- **Orphan transactions** - Transactions waiting for parent inputs\n- **Temporary files** - Protocol processing artifacts\n\n### Options\n- **Relative path** - Resolved relative to binary location\n- **Absolute path** - Full filesystem path\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use absolute paths in production for predictability\n- Ensure sufficient disk space for orphan transaction storage\n- Place on fast storage for better performance"`
	ListenAddresses                       []string      `key:"legacy_listen_addresses" desc:"Listen addresses for legacy Bitcoin protocol (pipe-separated)" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"For backward compatibility with older nodes" type:"[]string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nConfigures network addresses where the node accepts incoming connections from legacy Bitcoin protocol clients.\n\n### How It Works\nEnables backward compatibility with:\n- BSV Blockchain nodes not yet upgraded to Teranode\n- Legacy mining software\n- Traditional Bitcoin protocol tools\n\n### Options\n- **ip:port format** - Multiple addresses separated by pipes\n- **Empty string** - Disables legacy protocol listening entirely\n\n### Examples\n| Value | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| 0.0.0.0:8333 | Listen on all interfaces |\n| 192.168.1.10:8333 | Listen on specific IP |\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave empty unless you need to connect with legacy nodes\n- Use firewall rules to restrict access in production"`
	ConnectPeers                          []string      `key:"legacy_connect_peers" desc:"Legacy peers to connect to (pipe-separated)" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"For syncing with legacy Bitcoin nodes" type:"[]string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies legacy Bitcoin node addresses that this node should actively connect to as outbound peers.\n\n### How It Works\nThe node initiates connections to these addresses on startup and maintains persistent connections for:\n- Blockchain synchronization with legacy nodes\n- Transaction relay to legacy infrastructure\n- Block announcement propagation\n\n### Options\n- **ip:port format** - Multiple addresses separated by pipes\n- **Empty string** - No outbound legacy connections\n\n### Use Cases\n- Syncing with existing BSV Blockchain infrastructure\n- Connecting to known trusted legacy nodes\n- Private network setups with legacy components\n\n### Recommendations\n- Only connect to trusted legacy nodes\n- Monitor connection health in logs"`
	OrphanEvictionDuration                time.Duration `key:"legacy_orphanEvictionDuration" desc:"Duration before evicting orphan transactions" default:"10m" category:"Legacy" usage:"How long to keep orphan transactions" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how long orphan transactions are retained before being automatically removed from memory.\n\n### How It Works\nOrphan transactions are transactions received where one or more parent inputs are unknown. The node holds these temporarily hoping the parent transactions arrive soon.\n\nAfter this duration, orphans are evicted to:\n- Prevent memory exhaustion\n- Clean up transactions that will likely never complete\n- Protect against orphan flooding attacks\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Longer duration** - More time for parent transactions to arrive, but uses more memory\n- **Shorter duration** - Less memory usage, but may prematurely evict valid orphans\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 10 minutes works well for most networks\n- Reduce on memory-constrained systems\n- Increase if experiencing frequent orphan re-requests"`
	MaxOrphanTxs                          int           `key:"legacy_maxOrphanTxs" desc:"Maximum number of orphan transactions held in memory" default:"100" category:"Legacy" usage:"Hard cap on the orphan transaction pool" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds the size of the orphan transaction pool to prevent memory-exhaustion DoS from a peer flooding unique orphan transactions faster than the TTL eviction rate.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen the orphan pool reaches this cap, inserting a new orphan evicts the oldest entry first (by insertion time). The evicted orphan is given one final validation attempt, mirroring TTL-based eviction semantics.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 100 matches Bitcoin Core's historical orphan pool size.\n- Raise only if you have reason to expect legitimate bursts of orphans larger than the default and have memory headroom.\n- Setting to 0 disables the cap (legacy unbounded behaviour) and is not recommended on public networks."`
	StoreBatcherSize                      int           `key:"legacy_storeBatcherSize" desc:"Batch size for legacy store operations" default:"1024" category:"Legacy" usage:"Number of operations per batch" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the number of storage operations grouped together in a single batch when processing legacy protocol messages.\n\n### How It Works\nInstead of writing each item individually, operations are collected into batches:\n- Reduces storage round trips\n- Improves throughput for bulk data\n- Amortizes overhead across multiple operations\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Larger batches** - Better throughput, higher memory usage, increased latency per item\n- **Smaller batches** - Lower latency, more storage overhead, reduced throughput\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 1024 balances throughput and memory well\n- Increase for high-volume legacy traffic\n- Decrease if memory is constrained"`
	StoreBatcherConcurrency               int           `key:"legacy_storeBatcherConcurrency" desc:"Concurrency for legacy store batcher" default:"32" category:"Legacy" usage:"Parallel store batch operations" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of parallel workers processing storage batch operations for the legacy protocol.\n\n### How It Works\nMultiple workers can process independent batches simultaneously, enabling:\n- Parallel writes to storage backends\n- Better utilization of multi-core systems\n- Higher aggregate throughput\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher concurrency** - Increased throughput, more resource usage, potential storage contention\n- **Lower concurrency** - Reduced resource usage, lower throughput\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 32 works well for most deployments\n- Scale with available CPU cores and storage backend capacity\n- Monitor storage backend for signs of contention"`
	SpendBatcherSize                      int           `key:"legacy_spendBatcherSize" desc:"Batch size for legacy spend operations" default:"1024" category:"Legacy" usage:"Number of spends per batch" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the number of UTXO spend operations grouped together in a single batch when processing legacy protocol transactions.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen transactions from the legacy protocol spend UTXOs, the operations are batched for efficiency:\n- Multiple spends processed together\n- Reduced UTXO store round trips\n- Improved validation throughput\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Larger batches** - Better throughput for high-volume scenarios\n- **Smaller batches** - Lower latency for individual transactions\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep aligned with **StoreBatcherSize** for consistent behavior\n- Default of 1024 is suitable for typical workloads"`
	SpendBatcherConcurrency               int           `key:"legacy_spendBatcherConcurrency" desc:"Concurrency for legacy spend batcher" default:"32" category:"Legacy" usage:"Parallel spend batch operations" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of parallel workers processing UTXO spend batch operations for the legacy protocol.\n\n### How It Works\nMultiple workers process spend batches concurrently, enabling:\n- Parallel UTXO lookups and updates\n- Faster transaction validation\n- Better utilization of UTXO store capacity\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher concurrency** - Faster processing, more UTXO store load\n- **Lower concurrency** - Less resource contention, slower throughput\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep aligned with **StoreBatcherConcurrency** for consistent behavior\n- Default of 32 balances performance and resource usage"`
	OutpointBatcherSize                   int           `key:"legacy_outpointBatcherSize" desc:"Batch size for legacy outpoint operations" default:"1024" category:"Legacy" usage:"Number of outpoints per batch" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the number of outpoint lookup operations grouped together in a single batch when processing legacy protocol requests.\n\n### How It Works\nOutpoints (transaction output references) are looked up in batches for efficiency:\n- Multiple outpoint queries combined\n- Reduced UTXO store round trips\n- Improved lookup throughput\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Larger batches** - Better throughput for bulk lookups\n- **Smaller batches** - Lower latency for individual queries\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep aligned with other batcher sizes for consistent behavior\n- Default of 1024 is suitable for typical workloads"`
	OutpointBatcherConcurrency            int           `key:"legacy_outpointBatcherConcurrency" desc:"Concurrency for legacy outpoint batcher" default:"32" category:"Legacy" usage:"Parallel outpoint batch operations" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of parallel workers processing outpoint lookup batch operations for the legacy protocol.\n\n### How It Works\nMultiple workers process outpoint lookup batches concurrently, enabling:\n- Parallel UTXO queries\n- Faster transaction input resolution\n- Better utilization of UTXO store capacity\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher concurrency** - Faster lookups, more UTXO store load\n- **Lower concurrency** - Less resource contention, slower throughput\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep aligned with other batcher concurrency settings\n- Default of 32 balances performance and resource usage"`
	PrintInvMessages                      bool          `key:"legacy_printInvMessages" desc:"Print inventory messages for debugging" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for legacy protocol debugging" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables verbose logging of inventory (inv) messages received through the legacy Bitcoin protocol.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled, every inventory message is logged including:\n- Transaction announcements from peers\n- Block announcements\n- Other inventory vectors\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Log all inventory messages\n- **false** - Suppress inventory message logging\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Useful for debugging protocol issues, generates high log volume\n- **Disabled** - Cleaner logs, less visibility into protocol traffic\n\n### Recommendations\n- **Disable in production** - Very verbose output\n- Enable temporarily when troubleshooting legacy peer connectivity\n- Use in conjunction with log rotation when enabled"`
	GRPCAddress                           string        `key:"legacy_grpcAddress" desc:"gRPC address for Legacy service" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"Address for legacy service communication" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the gRPC address used by other Teranode services to communicate with the legacy service.\n\n### How It Works\nOther microservices connect to this address to:\n- Request data from legacy peers\n- Coordinate transaction propagation\n- Exchange blockchain state information\n\n### Options\n- **host:port format** - Full address for the gRPC endpoint\n- **Empty string** - Legacy service gRPC client disabled\n\n### Recommendations\n- Configure when running distributed multi-service deployments\n- Use internal network addresses for security\n- Ensure firewall allows traffic between services"`
	AllowBlockPriority                    bool          `key:"legacy_allowBlockPriority" desc:"Enable the BSV multistream BlockPriority stream policy for legacy P2P" default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"Negotiate multiple TCP streams per peer, separating block traffic from general traffic" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables the BSV multistream protocol (BlockPriority stream policy) for legacy P2P connections. Peers negotiate multiple TCP streams per association, separating bulk block/header transfer (DATA1) from latency-sensitive control traffic (GENERAL).\n\n### How It Works\n- Advertised in the protoconf handshake; peers that also support BlockPriority open a DATA1 stream\n- Block and header replies are delivered on DATA1, isolating multi-GB transfers from control messages\n- Required for reliable download of large (multi-GB) blocks from BSV mainnet sync peers\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Negotiate BlockPriority multistream with capable peers (default)\n- **false** - Single-stream legacy protocol only\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for mainnet/testnet sync, where large blocks require the dedicated block stream"`
	GRPCListenAddress                     string        `key:"legacy_grpcListenAddress" desc:"gRPC listen address for Legacy service" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"Port for incoming legacy gRPC connections" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the address where the legacy service listens for incoming gRPC connections from other Teranode services.\n\n### How It Works\nThe legacy service exposes its internal API on this address, allowing other microservices to:\n- Query legacy peer state\n- Submit transactions for legacy propagation\n- Access legacy-specific functionality\n\n### Options\n- **host:port format** - Address to bind the gRPC server\n- **Empty string** - Legacy gRPC server disabled\n\n### Recommendations\n- Bind to internal network interfaces only\n- Use **0.0.0.0:port** for container deployments\n- Pair with **GRPCAddress** for bidirectional communication"`
	SavePeers                             bool          `key:"legacy_savePeers" desc:"Persist legacy peer list to disk" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for peer persistence across restarts" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls whether discovered legacy peer addresses are saved to disk for use across node restarts.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled:\n- Peer addresses discovered during operation are written to disk\n- On restart, saved peers are loaded immediately\n- Reduces time needed to establish network connectivity\n\nPeer data is stored in the **WorkingDir** location.\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Persist peers to disk\n- **false** - Peers discovered fresh each startup\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Faster startup, requires disk write permissions\n- **Disabled** - Clean slate each start, no stale peer data\n\n### Recommendations\n- Enable for production nodes with stable peer networks\n- Disable for ephemeral/test deployments\n- Periodically clean peer database to remove stale entries"`
	AllowSyncCandidateFromLocalPeers      bool          `key:"legacy_allowSyncCandidateFromLocalPeers" desc:"Allow sync candidate from local peers" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for local network sync" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls whether peers on local/private networks are considered as blockchain sync candidates.\n\n### How It Works\nBy default, the node excludes local network peers (RFC1918 addresses) from sync candidate selection as a security measure. When enabled:\n- Local network peers can be chosen for initial block download\n- Private testnet setups work correctly\n- Development environments can sync without public peers\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Local network peers eligible for sync\n- **false** - Only public network peers eligible for sync\n\n### Use Cases\n- Private/isolated blockchain networks\n- Development and testing environments\n- Air-gapped deployments\n\n### Recommendations\n- **Keep disabled for mainnet** - Prevents local network attacks\n- Enable only for private networks and testing\n- Combine with **ConnectPeers** for controlled sync sources"`
	TempStore                             *url.URL      `key:"temp_store" desc:"Temporary storage URL for legacy operations" default:"file://./data/tempstore" category:"Legacy" usage:"Temporary data storage location" type:"url" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the storage location for temporary data generated during legacy protocol operations.\n\n### How It Works\nTemporary storage is used for:\n- Intermediate block processing data\n- Transaction validation scratch space\n- Protocol message buffering\n\n### Options\n- **file://path** - Local filesystem storage\n- Other URL schemes supported by the blob store\n\n### Examples\n| Value | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| file://./data/tempstore | Relative path (default) |\n| file:///var/teranode/temp | Absolute path |\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use fast storage (SSD) for better performance\n- Ensure sufficient space for block processing\n- Configure automatic cleanup or use tmpfs for ephemeral data"`
	PeerIdleTimeout                       time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerIdleTimeout" desc:"Timeout for idle legacy peers" default:"125s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Disconnect after this idle time (ping interval is 2 mins)" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the maximum time a legacy peer connection can remain idle before being disconnected.\n\n### How It Works\nThe Bitcoin protocol uses a 2-minute ping/pong mechanism to detect dead connections. This timeout:\n- Starts counting from last message received\n- Resets on any incoming protocol message\n- Triggers disconnection when exceeded\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher timeout** - More tolerant of network issues, slower detection of dead peers\n- **Lower timeout** - Faster cleanup of dead connections, may disconnect slow peers prematurely\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 125 seconds provides buffer above 2-minute ping interval\n- Account for network jitter and latency\n- Increase for high-latency network conditions\n- Do not set below 120 seconds (ping interval)"`
	PeerProcessingTimeout                 time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerProcessingTimeout" desc:"Timeout for processing legacy peer messages" default:"3m" category:"Legacy" usage:"Maximum time for processing block messages" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the maximum time allowed to process a single legacy protocol message before timing out.\n\n### How It Works\nSome protocol messages, particularly large blocks, can take significant time to process. This timeout:\n- Applies per-message during processing\n- Prevents indefinite hangs on malformed data\n- Protects against slow-loris style attacks\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher timeout** - Accommodates larger blocks, slower detection of stuck processing\n- **Lower timeout** - Faster failure detection, may timeout on legitimate large blocks\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 3 minutes handles most block sizes\n- Increase for networks with very large blocks\n- Monitor for timeout errors in logs to adjust\n- Consider hardware performance when tuning"`
	BlockPrefetchBufferBytes              int64         `key:"legacy_blockPrefetchBufferBytes" desc:"Memory budget (bytes) for blocks downloaded ahead of processing during legacy sync. 0 disables prefetch (synchronous ingestion)." default:"268435456" category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds the total serialized bytes of received-but-not-yet-processed blocks across all legacy peers" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDecouples the per-peer network read-loop from per-block processing during legacy/IBD sync so block download overlaps validation, while bounding the memory held by blocks that have been received but not yet processed.\n\n### How It Works\nThe read-loop normally blocks until each block finishes processing, so download never overlaps processing (the next block's ~seconds-long download is serialized after the previous block's validation). With a non-zero budget the read-loop instead admits a block against a global byte-weighted semaphore and continues reading the next block while the previous one is validated in the background. The budget is the hard ceiling on the total serialized size of in-flight (received, queued, or mid-validation) blocks across ALL legacy peers and streams. A single block larger than the whole budget is admitted alone (it waits for everything else to drain first), preserving the original one-block-at-a-time backpressure for huge blocks.\n\n### Options\n- **0** - Disable prefetch; fall back to the original synchronous ingestion (one block in flight, full TCP backpressure). Use as a kill switch.\n- **>0** - Enable bounded async prefetch with this byte budget.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Larger budget** - More download/processing overlap and higher small-block throughput, more resident memory for buffered blocks.\n- **Smaller budget** - Less memory, less overlap; at/below one block's size it degrades to synchronous behaviour.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 256 MiB hides the inter-block download gap in the small-block era while keeping buffered-block memory well bounded.\n- The weight is a block's serialized size; the live decoded object graph is a small multiple, so size the budget below the headroom you actually have.\n- Big-block era: a block at least as large as the whole budget is admitted alone (its weight is clamped to the budget), giving zero download/validation overlap — identical to pre-prefetch behaviour. To get overlap on large blocks, raise the budget to at least ~2x the typical block size so more than one is in flight.\n- Set to 0 to revert to proven synchronous behaviour if a regression is suspected."`
	Upnp                                  bool          `key:"legacy_upnp" desc:"Enable UPnP for automatic port mapping" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable automatic port forwarding via UPnP" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables Universal Plug and Play (UPnP) for automatic port forwarding on compatible routers.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled, the node will:\n- Attempt to discover UPnP-capable routers on the local network\n- Automatically configure port forwarding for incoming connections\n- Periodically renew the port mapping lease\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Enable UPnP port mapping\n- **false** - Disable UPnP (manual port forwarding required)\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Easier setup, requires UPnP support on router\n- **Disabled** - More secure, requires manual router configuration\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep disabled in production for security\n- Enable for home/development setups behind NAT\n- Ensure router supports UPnP if enabled"`
	ParallelWindowMemoryFraction          float64       `key:"legacy_parallelWindowMemoryFraction" desc:"Fraction of GOMEMLIMIT to use as the window byte budget (0=off)" default:"0" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable byte-budgeted window admission for below-checkpoint blocks" type:"float64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen > 0, enables the byte-budgeted ProcessBlockWindow path for below-checkpoint outpoint-only blocks. A window of K blocks is prepared concurrently and submitted to ProcessBlockWindow in one batch. Default 0 = disabled (flag-off = byte-identical to the per-block path)."`
	ParallelWindowParkAhead               bool          `key:"legacy_parallelWindowParkAhead" desc:"Park below-checkpoint window blocks that run ahead of block assembly instead of blocking the drain goroutine (requires window enabled + inFlightRefillInterval>0; false=off, current blocking behaviour)" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Non-blocking maturity gate: park far-ahead blocks and release them as block assembly advances" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen true, a below-checkpoint window block that is more than MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly ahead of block assembly is PARKED in a drain-goroutine-local buffer and the drain select loop continues immediately, instead of blocking the loop for up to 30s in the maturity poll. Parked blocks are released back into the window, ascending and contiguous with the committed tip, as block assembly advances (checked on the continuous-refill tick). Requires ParallelWindowMemoryFraction>0 and InFlightRefillInterval>0. Default false = the original blocking maturity wait (byte-identical)."`
	ParallelWindowParkedMemoryFraction    float64       `key:"legacy_parallelWindowParkedMemoryFraction" desc:"Fraction of GOMEMLIMIT bounding parked far-ahead block memory (0=count cap only)" default:"0.10" category:"Legacy" usage:"Byte ceiling on parked blocks; secondary to the count cap" type:"float64"`
	ParallelWindowMaxParkedBlocks         int           `key:"legacy_parallelWindowMaxParkedBlocks" desc:"Sanity ceiling on the number of parked far-ahead blocks; the byte budget (parallelWindowParkedMemoryFraction) is the real memory guard" default:"16384" category:"Legacy" usage:"Count ceiling on parked blocks; kept high so tiny fast-IBD blocks fill by BYTES not by count, avoiding a park-refusal storm that starves block fetch" type:"int"`
	WindowMaxBlocks                       int           `key:"legacy_windowMaxBlocks" desc:"Block-count cap on ONE commit window, decoupled from the MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly maturity ceiling. Sits BELOW the ceiling (default 16 with ceiling 20) so the WORK-DRIVEN window flush hands the committer a real batch — the postgres UNNEST batchers coalesce ~16 blocks' txs instead of one — while leaving runway for the next window to prefill while the current one commits (overlapping CPU-side prep with disk-side commit instead of the IBD commit sawtooth). 0 (or >= MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly) falls back to the ceiling (the pre-decoupling behaviour). Only shrinks the window when >0 and < MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly." default:"16" category:"Legacy" usage:"Commit-window width for the work-driven flush during below-checkpoint IBD" type:"int"`
	ParkRunwayByteSized                   bool          `key:"legacy_parkRunwayByteSized" desc:"Size the fetch-runway horizon (fetchRunwayHorizon) by the park's REMAINING byte room instead of the binary full→gate / else→full-static-cap clamp. false (default) = today's binary clamp (byte-identical). true = the horizon retreats smoothly toward the maturity gate as the park fills (span = maxBehind + clamp(remainingParkBytes/avgBlockSize, 1, ParallelWindowMaxParkedBlocks)), removing the fat-block span flap that floods fetch budget with un-parkable far-ahead blocks and churns the frontier re-fetch. Falls back to the full static cap when the park byte budget is disabled (ParallelWindowParkedMemoryFraction=0) or no size samples exist yet." default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Smooth, occupancy-aware fetch runway to stop fat-block park churn during below-checkpoint IBD" type:"bool"`
	SyncPeerSilentTicks                   int           `key:"legacy_syncPeerSilentTicks" desc:"Consecutive 30s zero-byte samples before a silent sync peer is rotated during headers-first IBD (0=disable; values 1 clamp to 2)" default:"2" category:"Legacy" usage:"Fast rotation of a totally silent sync peer during IBD; cuts checkpoint-stall dead air from ~3min toward ~1min" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring headers-first IBD the sync peer always owes data (headers below the final checkpoint or requested blocks), so consecutive 30-second samples with literally zero association bytes mean it is stalled. The previous only detector was the 3-minute last-block-time window — the dominant dead-air cost when a checkpoint block's direct-path delivery is lost (rotation is that path's only recovery).\n\n### How It Works\n- Counts consecutive sync-peer ticker samples (30s) where the association byte counter did not advance\n- Any byte movement (header replay after rotation, fat-block streaming on DATA1) resets the counter, so a slow-but-alive peer can never be churned\n- Self-backpressure (local validation behind) also resets the counter — our slowness is not the peer's fault\n- Fires only in headers-first mode; post-IBD quiet is normal and the network-speed detector applies instead\n\n### Options\n- **0** — disable (previous behaviour, 3-minute detection only)\n- **2** (default) — rotate after ~60-90s of total silence\n- Values below 2 clamp to 2 (the first silent sample after settling is legitimate)"`
	SyncPeerHeadersFirstStaleBlockTimeout time.Duration `key:"legacy_syncPeerHeadersFirstStaleBlockTimeout" desc:"How long the sync peer may go without COMPLETING a block before the last-block-time detector rotates it, DURING headers-first IBD only. Post-IBD stays at the 3-minute maxLastBlockTime. Kept no shorter than 3m (a smaller value falls back to 3m = today)." default:"30m" category:"Legacy" usage:"Stop rotating a healthy header-delivering sync peer mid-download during headers-first IBD" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring headers-first IBD the sync peer streams 80-byte headers and completes NO block for long stretches, so the 3-minute last-block-time window rotates a perfectly healthy header source mid-delivery. Because headers are single-sourced, that rotation freezes the header frontier for 30s-3min while a replacement is chosen — the dominant 'stick' during far-ahead sync. Widen the last-block-time window during headers-first so a delivering peer is not rotated for the expected no-blocks-complete condition.\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY in headers-first mode; post-IBD keeps the 3-minute maxLastBlockTime unchanged\n- A genuinely silent peer (zero association bytes) is still rotated in ~60-90s by the silence detector (SyncPeerSilentTicks), and an unanswered getheaders in ~90s by the peer-layer response deadline — so widening this does NOT let a dead header peer stall the frontier\n- The value is clamped to be no shorter than the 3-minute post-IBD window\n\n### Options\n- **30m** (default) — a delivering header peer is never rotated for no-blocks-complete; only a truly dead/silent one is (fast, by the other detectors)\n- **3m** (or any value <= 3m) — rollback to today's behaviour (byte-identical)"`
	InFlightTxBudget                      int           `key:"legacy_inFlightTxBudget" desc:"Transaction WORK budget bounding in-flight block fetches during IBD" default:"50000" category:"Legacy" usage:"How many average-sized (in txs) blocks may be requested in flight" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds the number of blocks requested in flight during headers-first (legacy IBD) block download by the WORK to process (transactions), not a fixed block count. A 1-tx block and a 3000-tx block cost wildly different amounts, so bounding by transactions keeps the sync peer's feed busy for tiny blocks while keeping fat blocks few-in-flight.\n\n### How It Works\n- maxInFlight = InFlightTxBudget / max(avgTxCount, 1), where avgTxCount is the rolling average transactions per block\n- Clamped to a minimum of 1 (always at least one in flight)\n- Clamped to a hard cap (1024) so tiny 1-tx blocks don't produce an absurd request count\n- Further clamped by **InFlightByteBudget** so in-flight memory stays bounded even when tx counts under-represent memory (few huge txs)\n\n### Recommendations\n- **50000** (default) ≈ 20 blocks of ~2500 txs, or thousands of tiny blocks (capped at 1024) — aggressive streaming for tiny-block IBD\n- Increase to stream more tiny blocks in flight; decrease to reduce peak in-flight work\n- Before any block sample exists the bound falls back to 20 in flight"`
	InFlightByteBudget                    int64         `key:"legacy_inFlightByteBudget" desc:"Byte safety clamp on in-flight block-fetch memory during IBD (0=derive 25% of GOMEMLIMIT)" default:"0" category:"Legacy" usage:"Hard upper bound on in-flight fetch bytes; protects memory when tx counts mislead" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nCaps the total bytes of blocks requested in flight during legacy IBD, as a safety clamp on top of the transaction WORK budget (**InFlightTxBudget**). When a block has few but very large transactions, the tx count under-represents memory; this clamp keeps in-flight memory bounded so it cannot blow the process memory ceiling.\n\n### How It Works\n- byteBound = InFlightByteBudget / max(avgSize, 1), where avgSize is the rolling average block size in bytes\n- The in-flight bound is min(tx-budget result, byte-budget result)\n- **0** (default) derives the budget as 25% of GOMEMLIMIT (6 GB fallback when unset), i.e. ~1.5 GB — a small fraction of the process budget leaving headroom for validation, the window path, and the runtime\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **0** to track GOMEMLIMIT automatically\n- Set an explicit byte value only to override the derived fraction (e.g. on memory-constrained hosts)"`
	InFlightRefillInterval                time.Duration `key:"legacy_inFlightRefillInterval" desc:"Interval between continuous-refill getdata top-ups during headers-first IBD (0=disable)" default:"20ms" category:"Legacy" usage:"How often the drain goroutine tops the in-flight block-fetch window back up to the cap" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDrives the continuous-refill ticker on the block-drain goroutine: every interval it tops the in-flight block-fetch window back up to the memory-aware cap, independently of per-block processing latency, so the sync peer's feed never idles between block completions during headers-first (legacy IBD) download.\n\n### How It Works\n- Only fires while headers-first mode is active; a no-op (cheap atomic load) once IBD completes\n- Issues getdata top-ups only (never re-arms getheaders) via the non-blocking send path, so a write-stalled peer cannot wedge the drain\n- **0** disables the ticker entirely (top-ups then happen only as a side effect of a block completing)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **20ms** for aggressive streaming during IBD; increase to reduce tick overhead, set 0 to disable"`
	ParallelFetchPeers                    int           `key:"legacy_parallelFetchPeers" desc:"Max peers to fetch blocks from in parallel during below-checkpoint headers-first IBD (1=single sync peer, today's behaviour)" default:"1" category:"Legacy" usage:"Number of peers to parallel-download blocks from; 1 disables (byte-identical to single-peer)" type:"int"`
	BlockDownloadWindow                   int           `key:"legacy_blockDownloadWindow" desc:"Max blocks to have in-flight ahead of the validated tip across all peers during below-checkpoint parallel IBD (Bitcoin Core BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW analog)" default:"1024" category:"Legacy" usage:"Download-ahead window; memory-capped separately from MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly" type:"int"`
	MaxBlocksInTransitPerPeer             int           `key:"legacy_maxBlocksInTransitPerPeer" desc:"Max block requests outstanding to a single peer during parallel IBD (Bitcoin Core MAX_BLOCKS_IN_TRANSIT_PER_PEER analog)" default:"16" category:"Legacy" usage:"Per-peer in-flight cap for parallel block download" type:"int"`
	BlockStallTimeout                     time.Duration `key:"legacy_blockStallTimeout" desc:"How long the lowest-height outstanding block may stay unfilled by its assigned peer before that peer is disconnected during parallel IBD (Bitcoin Core BLOCK_STALLING_TIMEOUT analog)" default:"2s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Head-of-line stall timeout; disconnects the peer holding the next-needed block and frees it for reassignment" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nUnder disjoint multi-peer block download (**ParallelFetchPeers > 1**) blocks commit in ascending height order, so if the peer assigned the NEXT-NEEDED (lowest-height outstanding) block stalls, the ordered commit stalls even though higher blocks have already arrived. This is the head-of-line pause the parallel scheduler exists to avoid.\n\n### How It Works\n- On each continuous-refill tick the drain goroutine finds the head (lowest-height) outstanding block via the header-height index\n- If it has been outstanding with its assigned peer longer than this timeout, ONLY that head peer is disconnected and all of its assignments are freed so the next scheduler tick reassigns them to other peers\n- Freed blocks are never lost: haveInventory still reports them missing so they are re-requested, and a late block from the disconnected peer is a harmless no-op\n- Only ever acts on the single head peer per tick (never mass-disconnects, never an honest peer still within the timeout)\n- Has no effect when **ParallelFetchPeers <= 1** (single-peer path unchanged)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **2s** to mirror Bitcoin Core's BLOCK_STALLING_TIMEOUT; increase on high-latency links, decrease to fail over from a stalled peer faster"`
	HeadStallDisconnectTimeout            time.Duration `key:"legacy_headStallDisconnectTimeout" desc:"How long the SAME head block may stay stalled — across re-fetch races to other peers — before the peer holding it is DISCONNECTED. At BlockStallTimeout the head block is first re-assigned to another peer (raced) WITHOUT a disconnect; only after this longer window of continuous stall is the peer disconnected. 0 = disconnect immediately at BlockStallTimeout (the pre-F1 behaviour, byte-identical rollback)." default:"30s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Race a stalled head block to other peers before disconnecting the sync peer, so head-of-line stalls stop churning the sync peer and resetting the headers-first download" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDisconnecting the peer that holds a stalled head block is expensive when that peer is the SYNC peer: it triggers updateSyncPeer -> a full headers-first reset, and repeated every 2s (BlockStallTimeout) on a slow frontier block that header-reset churn freezes the single-sourced header frontier for minutes (the mainnet IBD 'stick'). svnode instead re-requests a slow block from other peers in parallel and only drops the peer after a much longer window.\n\n### How It Works\n- At BlockStallTimeout the head block is re-enqueued for re-fetch so the scheduler hands it to a peer with spare capacity next tick — the current peer is NOT disconnected (race, don't kill)\n- The head block's total stall time is tracked ACROSS these reassignments (independent of the per-assignment timer, which resets each time)\n- Only if the SAME head block stays stuck longer than this window — i.e. the race genuinely failed, peers cannot deliver it — is the peer holding it disconnected and all its assignments freed\n- Has no effect when **ParallelFetchPeers <= 1** (single-peer path unchanged)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **30s** to give the re-fetch race time to rescue the block from another peer before the stronger disconnect action; this is the F1 head-of-line churn fix\n- Set to **0** to restore the pre-F1 behaviour (immediate disconnect at BlockStallTimeout)"`
	BlockStallMinRate                     int64         `key:"legacy_blockStallMinRate" desc:"Bytes/sec floor below which a sole-blocker head peer may be disconnected for head-of-line stalling (0=disable the rate gate, restoring pre-F2 behaviour)." default:"102400" category:"Legacy" usage:"Throughput gate on the residual head-of-line disconnect; a peer still moving bytes faster than this is never dropped for holding the head block" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEven after the F1 re-fetch race, a head block that no peer can deliver eventually disconnects the peer holding it. On mainnet IBD that fires against peers that are simply SLOW rather than dead — a fat frontier block genuinely takes longer than the stall window to transfer, and dropping the peer mid-transfer throws away the partial download and restarts it elsewhere, which is strictly slower. svnode never disconnects a peer that is actively delivering bytes.\n\n### How It Works\n- Before the head-of-line disconnect fires, the peer's recent association throughput is compared against this floor\n- A peer sustaining at least this many bytes/sec is delivering; the disconnect is skipped and the block is left to finish\n- Only a peer below the floor — i.e. genuinely stalled, not merely slow — is disconnected\n- Combined with **HeadStallDisconnectTimeout**, a peer must be BOTH stuck on the same head block for the whole window AND moving fewer than this many bytes/sec\n\n### Options\n- **102400** (default) — 100 KiB/s, well below any usable sync peer, so only truly stalled peers are dropped\n- **0** — disable the rate gate entirely (pre-F2 behaviour: disconnect purely on the timeout)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Raise only if you see peers trickling just above the floor indefinitely; lower it on genuinely thin links"`
	IBDBlockStallTimeout                  time.Duration `key:"legacy_ibdBlockStallTimeout" desc:"Peer-layer block stall deadline used WHILE CATCHING UP (headers-first IBD). Post-IBD keeps the tip value unchanged." default:"60m" category:"Legacy" usage:"How long a peer may go without completing a requested block during IBD before the peer layer kills the connection" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe peer-layer stall deadline is sized for the chain tip, where a block is expected within seconds of the getdata. During IBD it is routinely exceeded for entirely healthy reasons: the block is a multi-hundred-megabyte historical block, or our own validation backpressure delays the read loop. The peer is then killed mid-transfer and the partial download is discarded, which is the main source of the disconnect/reconnect churn during mainnet IBD.\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY while headers-first catch-up is active; at the tip the original (shorter) deadline still applies, so a dead tip peer is still detected fast\n- The detector is NOT disabled — it simply waits as long as svnode waits before concluding the peer is dead\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **60m**: long enough that no honest historical block transfer trips it, short enough that a genuinely wedged connection is still reaped\n- Set equal to the tip value to restore pre-F3 behaviour"`
	IBDHeadersStallTimeout                time.Duration `key:"legacy_ibdHeadersStallTimeout" desc:"Peer-layer headers stall deadline used WHILE CATCHING UP (headers-first IBD). Post-IBD keeps the tip value unchanged." default:"10m" category:"Legacy" usage:"How long a peer may go without answering a getheaders during IBD before the peer layer kills the connection" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring IBD a peer answering getheaders may legitimately pause: it is reading historical headers off cold storage, or it is busy serving the block stream we are also pulling from it. The tip-sized deadline treats that pause as death and disconnects the single peer sourcing our header frontier, freezing the frontier while a replacement is chosen.\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY while headers-first catch-up is active; at the tip the original deadline still applies\n- **HeaderDeliveryTimeout** remains the fast liveness clock underneath this, so widening this value cannot produce an unbounded header freeze\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **10m**; set equal to the tip value to restore pre-F3 behaviour"`
	IBDMaxBlockDownloadTime               time.Duration `key:"legacy_ibdMaxBlockDownloadTime" desc:"Maximum time a single block download may take WHILE CATCHING UP (headers-first IBD) before the peer layer aborts it. Post-IBD keeps the tip value unchanged." default:"60m" category:"Legacy" usage:"Wall-clock ceiling on one block transfer during IBD" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nA historical mainnet block can be hundreds of megabytes; on a shared link its transfer legitimately takes many minutes. The tip-sized ceiling aborts these transfers just before they complete and forces a full re-download from another peer, wasting the bandwidth already spent — the single most expensive failure mode during IBD.\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY while headers-first catch-up is active; at the tip the original ceiling still applies\n- This is a wall-clock ceiling on the whole transfer, distinct from **IBDBlockStallTimeout** which measures time since the last progress\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **60m**; set equal to the tip value to restore pre-F3 behaviour"`
	IBDPeerIdleTimeout                    time.Duration `key:"legacy_ibdPeerIdleTimeout" desc:"Idle-connection deadline used WHILE CATCHING UP (headers-first IBD). Post-IBD keeps PeerIdleTimeout unchanged." default:"20m" category:"Legacy" usage:"How long a peer connection may be silent during IBD before it is disconnected as dead" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\n**PeerIdleTimeout** is sized against the 2-minute ping/pong interval, which is the right clock at the tip. During IBD a non-sync peer we are not currently pulling from can be silent far longer than that without being dead, and disconnecting it costs a full handshake to re-establish supply — the churn this whole change set exists to remove.\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY while headers-first catch-up is active; at the tip **PeerIdleTimeout** applies unchanged\n- The connection is still reaped, just on svnode's timescale rather than the tip's\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **20m**; set equal to **PeerIdleTimeout** to restore pre-F3 behaviour"`
	HeaderDeliveryTimeout                 time.Duration `key:"legacy_headerDeliveryTimeout" desc:"Liveness clock on header DELIVERY: how long the header frontier may stand still with a getheaders outstanding before the sourcing peer is rotated (0=disable)" default:"90s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds the header freeze that the widened IBD deadlines would otherwise permit" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThis is the counterweight to the widened IBD deadlines (**IBDHeadersStallTimeout** and friends). Those make the node patient about a peer that is slow to answer; without a separate liveness clock that patience becomes a hazard, because headers are single-sourced: one peer that accepts a getheaders and never answers would freeze the header frontier — and therefore ALL block download — for the full widened window.\n\nThis setting measures the thing that actually matters: whether the frontier is MOVING. It is a delivery clock, not a connection clock.\n\n### How It Works\n- Starts when a getheaders is outstanding and the frontier height is unchanged\n- Any header batch that advances the frontier resets it\n- On expiry the header source is rotated (not the whole connection torn down where avoidable), so the frontier resumes from another peer within seconds\n- **0** disables the clock — do not do this while the IBD deadlines are widened, as it removes the only bound on a header freeze\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **90s**: comfortably above a slow-but-real getheaders round trip, far below the widened IBD deadlines it protects"`
	UnconnectingHeadersTolerance          int           `key:"legacy_unconnectingHeadersTolerance" desc:"Consecutive non-connecting/unrequested header batches tolerated from a peer before disconnecting (svnode MAX_UNCONNECTING_HEADERS=10; 0 or 1 = disconnect on the first, i.e. pre-F4 behaviour)." default:"10" category:"Legacy" usage:"Tolerate the benign header races that are routine during IBD instead of disconnecting on the first one" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring IBD a peer routinely sends headers that do not connect to anything we hold, or that we did not ask for: it announced a block while our own getheaders was in flight, or it is on a slightly different frontier than the one we last asked about. These are benign races, not misbehaviour. Disconnecting on the first one throws away a working peer for doing something svnode explicitly tolerates.\n\n### How It Works\n- Counts CONSECUTIVE non-connecting or unrequested header batches from a peer\n- Any batch that connects normally resets the counter, so a healthy peer never accumulates toward the limit\n- Only a peer that persistently sends garbage — which is what actual misbehaviour looks like — reaches the limit and is disconnected\n\n### Options\n- **10** (default) — matches svnode's MAX_UNCONNECTING_HEADERS\n- **0** or **1** — disconnect on the first such batch (pre-F4 behaviour)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **10**; the detector still fires, it just fires on real misbehaviour rather than on a race"`
	TolerateUnrequestedBlocksInIBD        bool          `key:"legacy_tolerateUnrequestedBlocksInIBD" desc:"During CATCHINGBLOCKS, drop an unrequested/duplicate block silently instead of disconnecting the peer that sent it (svnode tolerates these during IBD)." default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"Breaks the rotation-orphan disconnect cascade that re-anchors the header frontier during IBD" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen the sync peer is replaced, the header reset and requestedBlocks clear orphan the blocks that peer already had in flight. Those blocks then arrive after the ledgers no longer list them, and once their short recentlyNeededUntil grace expires — which happens routinely when block commit is slow (postgres-bound fat blocks) — the delivered-block handler treats a harmless late duplicate as spam and disconnects the peer for 'Got unrequested block'. Because that peer is usually the sync peer, the disconnect triggers another header reset, which orphans more in-flight blocks: a self-sustaining cascade that collapses the header frontier (measured live: 945000 down to the committed tip) and freezes the tip for minutes — the busy/quiet oscillation.\n\n### How It Works\n- Only while the FSM is in CATCHINGBLOCKS (IBD); at the chain tip an unrequested block is genuine misbehaviour and still disconnects\n- A block still in the live header index or the reset carryover is processed as before (unchanged)\n- Only a block that is neither requested nor still-needed is affected: during IBD it is dropped silently (no disconnect, no processing) and re-fetched by the normal loop if actually needed\n\n### Options\n- **true** (default) — drop silently during IBD, matching svnode; disconnect only at the tip\n- **false** — pre-fix behaviour: disconnect for any unrequested block regardless of sync state\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave **true**; the disconnect it removes does not stop abuse during IBD (the block is dropped either way) but does trigger the frontier-collapse cascade"`
	ReplenishInterval                     time.Duration `key:"legacy_replenishInterval" desc:"How often the connection manager tops outbound peers back up to the target" default:"2s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Connection supply cadence; faster replenish closes the gap left by a disconnect" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEvery disconnect leaves the node below its outbound target until the connection manager notices and dials a replacement. A slow replenish cadence turns each disconnect into a measurable stretch of reduced download capacity — visible during IBD as the 'quiet' half of the busy/quiet oscillation.\n\n### How It Works\n- On each tick the connection manager compares live outbound peers against the target and dials the shortfall\n- Faster ticks mean the shortfall is closed in seconds rather than tens of seconds\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **2s**; the cost of a faster tick is a cheap periodic comparison, the benefit is that connection supply tracks demand closely"`
	FeelerInterval                        time.Duration `key:"legacy_feelerInterval" desc:"How often to make a short-lived feeler connection to test an untried address from the address manager (0=disable)" default:"120s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Keeps the address book stocked with addresses known to be reachable" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWithout feelers the address book fills with addresses that have never been tested, so when a peer is lost the replacement dial is a coin flip and may fail several times before connecting. svnode runs feelers precisely so that replacement connections succeed on the first attempt.\n\n### How It Works\n- On each interval one untried address is dialled, the handshake completed, and the connection immediately closed\n- The outcome marks the address tried/good in the address manager, so future replacement dials draw from a vetted pool\n- Feelers do not count against the outbound peer target — they are transient by design\n\n### Options\n- **120s** (default) — matches svnode's feeler cadence\n- **0** — disable feelers entirely\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave enabled; the bandwidth cost is negligible and it directly shortens replacement time after a disconnect"`
	DNSReseedMinAddresses                 int           `key:"legacy_dnsReseedMinAddresses" desc:"Re-query the DNS seeds when the address manager holds fewer than this many addresses (0=disable re-seed)" default:"500" category:"Legacy" usage:"Refills the address book so the node cannot run out of candidates to dial" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDNS seeds are normally queried only at startup. On a long IBD the address book erodes — addresses are tried, fail, and are aged out — until there are too few candidates left to sustain the outbound target. The node then sits below target with no way to recover short of a restart.\n\n### How It Works\n- The address manager's population is checked periodically; if it falls below this floor the DNS seeds are queried again\n- **DNSReseedMinInterval** rate-limits this so the seeds are never hammered\n\n### Options\n- **500** (default) — a comfortable floor above the working set needed to sustain outbound connections\n- **0** — disable re-seeding (startup query only)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **500**; re-seeding is rare in practice and is the only automatic recovery from an exhausted address book"`
	DNSReseedMinInterval                  time.Duration `key:"legacy_dnsReseedMinInterval" desc:"Minimum time between DNS re-seed queries" default:"30m" category:"Legacy" usage:"Rate limit protecting the DNS seeds from repeated queries" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nGuards the DNS seed operators. If the address book stays below **DNSReseedMinAddresses** — for example because the host has no working outbound network — the re-seed condition holds continuously, and without this floor the node would query the seeds in a tight loop.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **30m**; lowering it provides no benefit, since a re-seed that did not help will not help more often"`
	FrontierLogInterval                   time.Duration `key:"legacy_frontierLogInterval" desc:"How often to log the headers-first frontier height, so header-frontier freezes can be measured directly from the log without a tip sampler (0=disable)" default:"10s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Measurement hook: makes header-frontier stalls visible in the log" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nA frozen header frontier is the failure mode most of these settings exist to prevent, and it was previously invisible: the log shows block activity, not header progress, so a frontier freeze could only be inferred indirectly by sampling the tip from outside the process. This emits the frontier height on a fixed cadence, so a freeze appears in the log as a run of identical heights with a timestamp on each — directly measurable after the fact.\n\n### How It Works\n- Logs only while headers-first catch-up is active; a no-op at the tip\n- Fixed cadence rather than on-change, so a STALLED frontier still produces lines (an on-change log would go silent exactly when the problem occurs)\n\n### Options\n- **10s** (default) — fine enough to time a freeze, coarse enough to be cheap\n- **0** — disable the log line\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave enabled while IBD behaviour is being tuned"`
	BlockInFlightTimeout                  time.Duration `key:"legacy_blockInFlightTimeout" desc:"Max time ANY block (not just the head) may stay outstanding to its assigned peer before it is freed for re-fetch during parallel IBD (Bitcoin Core BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT analog)" default:"10s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Per-block download timeout; frees stuck blocks so the in-flight budget cannot be pinned by non-delivering peers" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nUnder disjoint multi-peer block download (**ParallelFetchPeers > 1**) the shared in-flight budget can be consumed by blocks assigned to peers that accept the getdata but never deliver. BlockStallTimeout only rescues the single HEAD block; non-head stuck blocks would otherwise pin the budget until the 60s requestedBlocks TTL expires, so the scheduler stops assigning, the head re-fetch cannot drain, and the download freezes for up to a minute. This bounds that freeze.\n\n### How It Works\n- On each continuous-refill tick, any block outstanding to its assigned peer longer than this timeout is freed (re-enqueued for re-fetch) so the budget frees and the block is re-requested, typically from a more responsive peer\n- Unlike BlockStallTimeout it does NOT disconnect the peer — it only frees the individual stuck block, so a merely-slow peer is not churned\n- Freed blocks are never lost: they re-enter the re-fetch set and are re-requested next tick\n- Has no effect when **ParallelFetchPeers <= 1** (single-peer path unchanged)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **10s** as a safety bound on the worst-case freeze; lower it (e.g. 3-5s) for tighter continuity at the cost of occasionally re-requesting a block a slow-but-honest peer would have delivered"`
	MaxBlockParallelFetch                 int           `key:"legacy_maxBlockParallelFetch" desc:"Max peers concurrently racing ONE slow frontier block (svnode DEFAULT_MAX_BLOCK_PARALLEL_FETCH). 1 = disable additive racing (byte-identical rollback)." default:"3" category:"Legacy" usage:"Number of peers a slow head/frontier block may be raced across, first-delivery-wins; 1 disables racing" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nA single slow peer holding the next-needed (contiguous frontier) block gates the whole in-order commit pipeline: successors pile into the park, cannot commit past the gap, get dropped as stray and re-fetched, while downstream validation and assembly sit idle. svnode avoids this by racing the SAME slow block across several peers, first-delivery-wins (BlockDownloadTracker keyed by {blockhash, nodeid}, DEFAULT_MAX_BLOCK_PARALLEL_FETCH = 3).\n\n### How It Works\n- Applies ONLY in the head-stall path once a head block has been in flight at least **BlockSlowFetchTimeout** (~30s); the disjoint forward walk and the delivery side are untouched\n- ADDITIVE: the current holder(s) are kept and up to this many peers total may hold the same block concurrently, one added per refill tick, naturally spaced by **InFlightRefillInterval**\n- First delivery clears the block from every racer's ledger; a losing racer's late copy fails the per-peer auth gate and is dropped silently during IBD (no disconnect) — this is the first-wins reconciliation\n- A holder still delivering above **BlockStallMinRate** is never raced (it is alive, just slow); racing sits on TOP of the staller-disconnect backstop for dead/malicious peers\n- The effective cap is clamped to min(**ParallelFetchPeers**, **MaxBlocksInTransitPerPeer**) so it can never exceed available peers or the per-peer in-flight budget\n\n### Options\n- **3** (default) — svnode-faithful; inert until an operator sets **ParallelFetchPeers > 1**\n- **1** — disable additive racing entirely; the head-stall path reverts to today's proven sequential single re-hand (F1), byte-identical. This is the in-deployment fine-grained rollback lever on a live financial node.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **3**; the outer kill switch is **ParallelFetchPeers <= 1** (whole subsystem off), the inner is setting this to 1"`
	BlockSlowFetchTimeout                 time.Duration `key:"legacy_blockSlowFetchTimeout" desc:"How long a head block stays in flight before an ADDITIONAL racer getdata may be issued to other peers (svnode DEFAULT_BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_SLOW_FETCH_TIMEOUT)." default:"30s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Age a head/frontier block must reach before it is eligible to be raced across extra peers" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe boundary of the two-speed head-stall response. Below this age a stalled head block is handled by today's proven fast MOVE (re-hand to one other peer at BlockStallTimeout, ~2s); at this age it becomes eligible for svnode-style additive RACING across up to **MaxBlockParallelFetch** peers. The two mechanisms never fight: the head block's total stall time keeps accumulating on the same hash, so once past this threshold the fast move is never used again for that block.\n\n### How It Works\n- Measured as the age of the oldest in-flight holder of the head block (true in-flight age across reassignments)\n- Below this: the sub-30s fast swap (measured mainnet-stick fix) runs — one holder, moved not raced\n- At/above this AND the holder is under **BlockStallMinRate** (genuinely slow, not merely alive): a single additional racer is added per refill tick, keeping the existing holder(s), up to the clamped cap\n- Mirrors svnode's slow-fetch eligibility gate; layered under the longer **HeadStallDisconnectTimeout** staller-disconnect backstop\n- Has no effect when **ParallelFetchPeers <= 1** or **MaxBlockParallelFetch = 1** (racing never entered)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **30s** to mirror svnode; raise to be more patient before spending extra peers' bandwidth on a duplicate fetch, lower to race a slow frontier block sooner at the cost of more duplicate downloads"`
	// DownloadToDisk gates the svnode-style download-to-disk decoupling of the
	// legacy IBD block pipeline. Default false = today's behaviour, byte-for-byte
	// (a live no-rebuild rollback lever).
	DownloadToDisk bool `key:"legacy_downloadToDisk" desc:"Master gate: write every fetched block to the durable block store on arrival (svnode AcceptBlock 'Store to disk') and drive the fetch frontier off on-disk presence, so a slow-validating giant block never stalls the fetcher and on-disk/restart-recovered blocks are never re-downloaded. false (default) = today's in-memory park/prefetch behaviour, byte-identical." default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable download-to-disk decoupling of the legacy IBD pipeline; roll out one node at a time" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring mainnet IBD over huge blocks (123k txs, 100-200MB, ~3.9s/subtree to validate) Teranode's legacy netsync freezes-then-bursts: a fetched block is held in memory against a prefetch byte-budget and the single drain goroutine runs the heavy prepare inline in arrival order, so a giant block back-pressures the whole pipeline and the fetcher stalls. svnode instead writes every fetched block to a flat file on arrival, marks it hasData(), and validates asynchronously in-order reading the block back from disk — so a giant block ties up exactly one validation slot and can never freeze fetch, message handling, or the persistence of any other block; on-disk blocks are also recognised across restarts and never re-downloaded.\n\n### What flipping this to true changes\n- **On arrival**: after the existing ban-check and authoritative header-height resolution (the stateless gate is KEPT), the raw block bytes are written to the existing durable mainBlockStore (fileformat.FileTypeBlock, keyed by block hash) exactly as block_persister does — idempotent, so a re-arrival or restart is a cheap no-op.\n- **Fetch frontier**: before requesting a header's block the scheduler skips anything already on disk (the hasData() analogue), so a slow-validating or restart-recovered block is never re-fetched. Composes with the existing per-peer in-flight ledger.\n- **In-memory park retired**: the bounded in-memory park never drops an on-disk block (the disk is the unbounded buffer, svnode's mapBlocksUnlinked); the fetch runway is bounded instead by a disk-ahead ceiling (legacy_downloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead) that THROTTLES fetch rather than dropping.\n- The heavy prepare runs on a separate in-order validation worker that reads blocks back from disk.\n\n### Options\n- **false** (default) — today's behaviour, byte-identical rollback.\n- **true** — download-to-disk decoupling.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Requires a configured block store (settings Block.BlockStore); if none is configured the feature stays off. Roll out on one node (teratestnet) at a time and watch the freeze-then-burst repro before flipping the default."`

	// DownloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead is the download-to-disk fetch ceiling: the
	// maximum number of blocks the fetch frontier may run ahead of the committed
	// (block-assembly) tip while legacy_downloadToDisk is on. It replaces the
	// retired in-memory park's count/byte cap as the bound on how much unvalidated
	// data accumulates on disk. Only consulted when legacy_downloadToDisk is true.
	DownloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead int `key:"legacy_downloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead" desc:"Download-to-disk fetch ceiling: max blocks the fetch frontier may run ahead of the committed (block-assembly) tip. When reached the fetcher THROTTLES (stops issuing getdata) until the in-order disk validator drains the backlog — an on-disk block is NEVER dropped. Only used when legacy_downloadToDisk is true. 0 = unbounded (not recommended). Default 288." default:"288" category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds unvalidated on-disk backlog during download-to-disk IBD; throttles fetch, never drops" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWith legacy_downloadToDisk on, fetched blocks land on disk (unbounded, svnode's flat-file buffer) and validate asynchronously in-order, so the bounded in-memory park that used to cap far-ahead blocks is retired. This setting is the replacement bound: it caps how far the fetch frontier may run ahead of the committed tip, and thus how much downloaded-but-unvalidated block data accumulates on disk during a long validation lag (e.g. a run of giant blocks).\n\n### How It Works\n- The fetch runway horizon becomes committedTip + this many blocks. A header whose height exceeds the horizon is not requested; the walk pauses there until the in-order disk validator commits blocks and the committed tip advances, sliding the horizon up.\n- Unlike the retired park, hitting the ceiling NEVER drops a block already on disk — it only stops issuing new getdata (svnode's nDownloadHeightThreshold throttle, not a drop).\n\n### Choosing a value\nDo NOT copy svnode's literal lower-window constant of 10 — it is documented there as a pruning-node restriction and is far too tight (it would re-couple fetch to validation speed). Size this from a disk budget: worst-case disk use ≈ this × average block size. At the mainnet big-block range (100-200MB) 288 blocks is up to ~40-60GB of transient on-disk backlog before fetch throttles; on typical small blocks it is negligible. svnode's loose upper reordering window is 1024 (legacy_blockDownloadWindow) — treat that as the ceiling of the sane range and 288 (a few hundred) as a conservative default that still fully decouples fetch from a slow validator.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **288** (default) — a few hundred blocks of look-ahead; bounds disk while keeping fetch far ahead of validation.\n- Raise toward 1024 if disk is plentiful and you want maximum reordering tolerance; lower if disk is tight."`

	// DownloadToDiskArrivalDAHWindow is the self-clean window (in blocks) applied
	// as a Delete-At-Height to every block written on arrival while
	// legacy_downloadToDisk is on. A block that never commits (a losing fork,
	// download-ahead discarded by a reorg, a peer's off-chain block) would
	// otherwise sit on disk forever on a non-pruning node; tagging the arrival
	// write with DAH = blockHeight + this window lets the store's janitor reap it
	// once the committed chain passes that height. A block that DOES commit has its
	// DAH cleared to 0 (permanent) at commit, so the retained chain is byte-for-byte
	// today's. Only consulted when legacy_downloadToDisk is true; 0 disables the
	// arrival DAH (blocks are then only removed by the delete-and-refetch recovery,
	// never self-expired). Defaults to GlobalBlockHeightRetention so unused
	// downloads expire on the same horizon the rest of the node retains blob data.
	DownloadToDiskArrivalDAHWindow int `key:"legacy_downloadToDiskArrivalDAHWindow" desc:"Self-clean window in blocks: an arrival block written under legacy_downloadToDisk is tagged with a Delete-At-Height of blockHeight + this many blocks, so a block that never commits (losing fork / download-ahead discarded by a reorg / off-chain block) self-expires once the committed chain passes that height. A committed block has its DAH cleared to 0 (permanent). Only used when legacy_downloadToDisk is true. 0 disables the arrival DAH. Default: GlobalBlockHeightRetention." category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds disk left by downloaded-but-never-committed blocks; committed chain is retained unchanged" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDownload-to-disk writes every fetched block to the durable block store on arrival. Blocks that go on to commit are the artifact the node keeps anyway; but a block written on arrival that NEVER commits — a losing fork, look-ahead data discarded by a reorg, a peer's off-chain block — would sit on disk forever on a node whose block store is not pruning. This is the self-clean bound: it mirrors block_persister's DAH pattern.\n\n### How It Works\n- On the arrival write the block is tagged with DAH = its own header height + this window. The store's janitor reaps a blob once the store's current height passes its DAH. Because the chain cannot pass a height without committing SOME block there, only blocks whose height was filled by a DIFFERENT (committed) block — i.e. orphans/losers — are ever reaped; a legitimately-ahead block commits (and clears its DAH to 0) long before the tip reaches its DAH.\n- On commit the DAH is cleared to 0 (permanent), exactly as block_persister does, so the committed chain is retained precisely as today.\n\n### Choosing a value\nKeep it comfortably larger than legacy_downloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead so a legitimately far-ahead block is never at risk of being reaped before it commits. The default (GlobalBlockHeightRetention) matches the horizon the rest of the node already uses to retain blob data.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **GlobalBlockHeightRetention** (default) — same retention horizon as the rest of the node.\n- **0** — disable the arrival DAH (rely solely on delete-and-refetch recovery to remove junk); not recommended on a non-pruning node." default:"0"`

	// DownloadStore is the URL of the DEDICATED, transient download buffer used by
	// the legacy_downloadToDisk path — a SEPARATE blob.Store instance from the
	// shared Block.BlockStore. It holds the full raw wire block keyed by hash while
	// the block is downloaded-but-not-yet-committed, and manages its own lifetime
	// (deleted on genuine commit, self-expiring via arrival DAH otherwise). Keeping
	// it separate from the shared block store avoids colliding with the COMPACT
	// FileTypeBlock that block_persister writes post-commit. Only consulted when
	// legacy_downloadToDisk is true; if nil/unconstructable the feature degrades off
	// (downloadToDisk() nil-guards the store).
	DownloadStore *url.URL `key:"legacy_downloadStore" desc:"Dedicated download buffer for the legacy_downloadToDisk path: a SEPARATE blob store instance from the shared blockstore, holding the full raw wire block keyed by hash while it is downloaded-but-not-yet-committed. Deleted on commit, self-expiring via arrival DAH otherwise. Default file://./data/legacy-download-blocks. Only used when legacy_downloadToDisk is true; nil/unconstructable = feature off." default:"file://./data/legacy-download-blocks" category:"Legacy" usage:"Transient raw-block download buffer for download-to-disk IBD; kept separate from the shared blockstore to avoid the compact-vs-full FileTypeBlock collision" type:"url" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe legacy_downloadToDisk path writes every fetched block to disk on arrival and validates asynchronously in-order. Those raw wire blocks must not share a store/key space with the COMPACT FileTypeBlock that block_persister writes after commit, so download-to-disk uses this DEDICATED store instance instead of the shared Block.BlockStore.\n\n### How It Works\n- Constructed as a minimal file-backed blob store (no block-height tracker / deletion scheduler): the buffer self-manages deletion — Del on genuine commit, arrival DAH for blocks that never commit.\n- Keyed by block hash (fileformat.FileTypeBlock), holding the full serialized wire block.\n\n### Options\n- **file://path** (default file://./data/legacy-download-blocks) — local filesystem buffer distinct from the blockstore.\n- **empty / unconstructable** — the download-to-disk feature degrades off (the store nil-guard in downloadToDisk()).\n\n### Recommendations\n- Point at fast local storage with room for the transient backlog (worst-case ≈ legacy_downloadToDiskMaxBlocksAhead × average block size).\n- Keep it on a DIFFERENT path from the shared blockstore."`

	// DiskPrepareWorkers is the number of parallel prepare workers on the
	// download-to-disk in-order validator path (Wave 2 #4). The heavy
	// prepareBlockForWindow body (subtree partition/extend/write + stateless
	// PoW/merkle/CVE-2012-2459 checks) is per-block independent, so a POOL of
	// workers prepares a sliding window of the next N uncommitted, on-disk,
	// mature headers concurrently; a height-keyed reorder buffer on the drain
	// goroutine re-serialises their out-of-order completions back into
	// strict-ascending, contiguous-with-committed-tip order before the window
	// accumulator, so commit ORDER and validation are unchanged. A fat block then
	// ties up ONE worker while its lower neighbours prepare and wait in the
	// reorder buffer, instead of freezing the single-worker commit pipeline.
	// Kept well below the maturity runway (MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly). 1 =
	// today's single prepare worker (byte-identical). Only consulted when
	// legacy_downloadToDisk is true and the window path is enabled.
	DiskPrepareWorkers int `key:"legacy_diskPrepareWorkers" desc:"Parallel prepare workers on the download-to-disk in-order validator (Wave 2). The per-block-independent prepareBlockForWindow body runs across this many workers on a sliding window of the next N uncommitted, on-disk, mature headers; a height-keyed reorder buffer feeds the commit accumulator in strict-ascending order, so commit order/validation are unchanged. A fat block occupies one worker while neighbours prepare, instead of freezing the single-worker pipeline. Keep well below MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly. 1 = single worker (byte-identical). Only used when legacy_downloadToDisk is true and the window path is enabled." default:"4" category:"Legacy" usage:"Parallel block-prepare pool for download-to-disk IBD; reorder buffer keeps commit strictly ascending" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nOn the download-to-disk validator a single prepare worker means a fat block (a 24k-tx block prepared for ~12s) monopolises the prepare slot and the single-worker commit pipeline drains behind it (a measured 110s dead-stall). prepareBlockForWindow is per-block independent — it reads bytes from the blob store and touches only concurrency-safe subtree/UTXO stores, never the drain-owned header/window/checkpoint state — so it is safe to run N-parallel with no added guarding.\n\n### How It Works\n- A pool of this many workers consumes prepare requests off a shared buffered channel and returns prepared blocks.\n- The drain goroutine dispatches a SLIDING WINDOW of the next N uncommitted, on-disk, mature headers (anchored at the download frontier) and collects completions into a height/hash-keyed reorder map.\n- It feeds the window accumulator ONLY the lowest-needed contiguous height as each completes, so wa.add still receives strict-ascending, contiguous-with-committed-tip input exactly as the depth-1 walk produced it. The existing drainJob sort/dedupe, contiguity gate and single FIFO poison-latched flush worker are untouched — parallelism lives ONLY in prepare-completion order, which the reorder buffer re-serialises to ascending before commit.\n- The window is bounded to N positions from the frontier, so at most N prepared blocks are ever in flight or buffered.\n\n### Options\n- **4** (default) — a handful of workers, far below the maturity runway.\n- **1** — the single prepare worker (byte-identical to before this change).\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep between 3 and 4, and always well below MaxBlocksBehindBlockAssembly so the reorder buffer never holds more than the runway's worth of out-of-order results.\n- Values above the maturity runway are clamped down to it; values below 1 clamp to 1." type:"int"`

	// SubtreeWriteConcurrency bounds how many of a block's subtrees are written
	// to the subtree store CONCURRENTLY (Wave 2 #5). Each subtree is an
	// independent content-addressed output (three files keyed by its own
	// RootHash), so the outer write loop in prepareSubtrees fans out across this
	// many subtrees at once instead of writing them one at a time; writeSubtree
	// still parallelises its own three internal file writes underneath, so the
	// peak concurrent blob writes is roughly this value times three. First-error
	// propagation and per-subtree Abort-on-failure are preserved by the errgroup.
	// Ordering is irrelevant (subtrees are independent) — the merkle-ordered
	// RootHash collection is a separate serial loop. 1 = the old serial loop
	// (byte-identical). Clamped to a floor of 1.
	SubtreeWriteConcurrency int `key:"legacy_subtreeWriteConcurrency" desc:"How many of a block's subtrees are written to the subtree store concurrently on the legacy sync path (Wave 2 #5). Each subtree is an independent content-addressed output, so the outer write loop fans out across this many at once (writeSubtree still parallelises its own three internal file writes underneath). First-error propagation and per-subtree Abort-on-failure are preserved. 1 = the old serial loop (byte-identical). Clamped to a floor of 1." default:"8" category:"Legacy" usage:"Concurrent subtree writes per block during legacy sync; overlaps blob I/O for many-subtree blocks" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nA large legacy block partitions into many subtrees, each written as three independent content-addressed files (subtree, subtreeData, subtreeMeta) keyed by the subtree's own RootHash. Writing them one subtree at a time serialises otherwise-independent blob I/O and lengthens prepare for fat blocks. This fans the outer loop out so multiple subtrees write at once.\n\n### How It Works\n- The outer subtree-write loop in prepareSubtrees runs under a bounded errgroup with this limit.\n- Each subtree's writeSubtree still parallelises its own three file writes, so peak concurrent blob writes is roughly this value times three.\n- First-error propagation (errgroup returns the first non-nil error) and per-write Abort-on-failure are preserved; content-addressed keys mean no cross-subtree collision and idempotent re-writes.\n\n### Options\n- **8** (default) — a handful of subtrees in flight, matching the writeSubtree internal fan-out.\n- **1** — the old serial loop (byte-identical to before Wave 2 #5).\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep between 4 and 8; higher values raise peak blob-store load for little gain on typical subtree counts.\n- Values below 1 clamp to 1." type:"int"`

	// DiskPrepareMemoryFraction bounds, by estimated bytes, the download-to-disk
	// prepare pool's combined in-flight-dispatch + reorder-buffer memory (Wave 2
	// review fix #1). DiskPrepareWorkers already bounds this by COUNT, but a
	// count-only cap says nothing about SIZE: N fat blocks (a 123k-tx block is
	// cited elsewhere in this file) held in flight/buffered at once can exceed
	// the memory the window byte budget is meant to cap. This adds a byte
	// ceiling alongside the existing count ceiling, mirroring the window
	// accumulator's own byte budget (windowBudgetBytes). 0 disables the byte
	// gate, leaving the count cap as the sole limit (pre-fix behaviour). Only
	// consulted when legacy_downloadToDisk is true and the window path is
	// enabled.
	DiskPrepareMemoryFraction float64 `key:"legacy_diskPrepareMemoryFraction" desc:"Fraction of GOMEMLIMIT bounding the download-to-disk prepare pool's combined in-flight + reorder-buffer bytes (0=count-cap only)" default:"0.15" category:"Legacy" usage:"Byte ceiling on the parallel prepare pool's sliding window; secondary to and independent of DiskPrepareWorkers' count cap" type:"float64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDiskPrepareWorkers bounds the parallel prepare pool's sliding window by COUNT (at most N blocks ever in flight or buffered in the reorder map), but a count cap alone does not bound memory: N simultaneously-held fat blocks can exceed the memory the window byte budget is meant to protect. This adds a byte ceiling that THROTTLES new dispatches (never the feed of the already-lowest header) once the pool's tracked bytes would exceed budget.\n\n### How It Works\n- A not-yet-completed in-flight prepare is tracked by an ESTIMATE: the running average of this session's completed block sizes, seeded (before any sample exists) by budget/DiskPrepareWorkers — an even split of the whole budget across a full window.\n- A completed, buffered entry is tracked by its ACTUAL size (wire.MsgBlock.SerializeSize, a cheap sum of per-tx sizes with no re-encode).\n- Before dispatching a NEW look-ahead candidate the drain checks whether the tracked total would exceed budget; if so it stops dispatching further candidates this pass (a smaller effective window), but ALWAYS dispatches the lowest uncommitted header regardless of budget — the byte gate only throttles how far AHEAD of it the pool runs, so the walk can never stall waiting on its own gate.\n\n### Options\n- **0.15** (default) — 15% of GOMEMLIMIT (or the 6 GB fallback when unset) as the combined prepare-pool budget.\n- **0** — disable the byte gate; DiskPrepareWorkers' count cap alone bounds the window (the pre-fix behaviour).\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at the default unless profiling shows the prepare pool itself is a memory contributor distinct from the commit window's own budget (ParallelWindowMemoryFraction)."`

	// SubtreeWriteMaxConcurrency is a SINGLE process-wide ceiling on the total
	// concurrent subtree-file blob writes across every prepare worker and every
	// block being prepared at once (Wave 2 review fix #2). DiskPrepareWorkers x
	// SubtreeWriteConcurrency x 3 (writeSubtree's own internal fan-out) can reach
	// dozens of concurrent blob writes with today's defaults; on a network-backed
	// subtree store, or an fd/connection-limited or saturated disk, that can
	// exhaust the store's connection pool or the process's file-descriptor
	// budget and turn store errors into prepare failures. This caps the
	// COMBINED total regardless of how many workers x subtrees are active,
	// superseding the naive product. Only wired (non-nil) when
	// legacy_downloadToDisk is true, so the flag-off path never acquires it.
	SubtreeWriteMaxConcurrency int `key:"legacy_subtreeWriteMaxConcurrency" desc:"Process-wide ceiling on TOTAL concurrent subtree-file blob writes across every download-to-disk prepare worker and block, superseding the DiskPrepareWorkers x SubtreeWriteConcurrency x 3 product" default:"16" category:"Legacy" usage:"Joint concurrency cap protecting the subtree blob store's connection pool / fd budget under the parallel prepare pool" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDiskPrepareWorkers (parallel prepare pool width) and SubtreeWriteConcurrency (per-block subtree fan-out) each bound their own dimension, but they MULTIPLY: with the Wave 2 defaults (4 workers x 8 subtrees x writeSubtree's own 3-way internal fan-out) up to ~96 concurrent subtree-file writes can be in flight at once, versus 3 before Wave 2. On a network-backed subtree store, or an fd/connection-limited or saturated disk, that can exhaust the store's write-connection pool or the process's file-descriptor budget, turning store errors into prepare failures — a self-inflicted throughput cliff.\n\n### How It Works\n- A single counting semaphore, owned by the SyncManager and shared across ALL prepare workers and ALL blocks, is acquired around each individual subtree-file write (writeSubtree's three g.Go bodies: the subtree file, the subtree-data file, the subtree-meta file) and released immediately when that one write finishes (success, error, or abort).\n- Because each write acquires exactly one permit and never waits on another permit of the SAME semaphore while holding one, the gate cannot deadlock — a flat counting semaphore acquired per-write-unit and released promptly.\n- The semaphore's Acquire is context-aware: if a sibling write in the same errgroup fails first, the group's context cancels and any writer still waiting for a permit returns promptly with that cancellation instead of blocking, preserving first-error propagation and per-subtree Abort-on-failure.\n- Only constructed when legacy_downloadToDisk is true; off the gate the field stays nil and every write proceeds exactly as before (byte-identical flag-off).\n\n### Options\n- **16** (default) — comfortably covers the Wave 2 default product's typical working set while protecting a modestly-provisioned local disk or store connection pool.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Raise on a high-fd-budget host with a fast, highly concurrent store; lower on a saturated disk or a store with a small connection pool.\n- Keep at or below DiskPrepareWorkers x SubtreeWriteConcurrency x 3 — a higher value never increases concurrency beyond what those two already allow, it only adds slack." type:"int"`
}
